course: dictionary
lesson: 3779
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: The word fool can be a noun, a verb or an adjective, so it can mean different things. Context tells you which meaning is right. «Don't be a fool» means don't act silly. Here fool is a noun, a person who acts without good sense.
explain: «You can't fool me» uses fool as a verb. It means I won't be tricked. Check by swapping in trick: «You can't trick me» still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
explain: Words can also show feelings. Tadhg and Cian are friends, and «Not bad, ya sap» is said fondly, not unkindly. Sap is Irish slang for a softie or a fool, and the friendly tone tells you it is a joke between friends.
fact: Fool can be a noun (a silly person), a verb (to trick someone) or an adjective (foolish).
fact: To fool someone means to trick them into believing something that is not true.
fact: To fool around means to behave in a silly way or play about, and «fooling around» near water, roads or stairs can cause accidents.
fact: «Eejit» means a fool in Irish slang, and «sap» means a softie or a fool, said fondly.
fact: In the UK, a fool is also a cold pudding of fruit and cream.
exercise: «He collapses in a heap in the road, groaning at what a fool he has been to strut about singing instead of hiding till dark.» What does fool mean here? | A cold pudding of fruit and cream | To trick someone into believing something untrue | Clever and careful | *A person who acted in a silly way
exercise: «You can't fool me.» What does fool mean in this sentence? | *To trick someone | To play about in a silly way | To be very clever | A cold pudding of fruit and cream
exercise: Find and copy the word that means a fool in Irish slang. | *eejit | April | tradition | midday
test: In the UK, what is a fool? | A kind of sweet drink | A sort of hat worn at parties | *A cold pudding of fruit and cream | A type of loud horn
test: «Stop fooling around near the water.» What does fooling around mean? | Trying to swim as fast as you can | *Behaving in a silly way or playing about | Shouting for help | Learning a new lesson
exam: «The wish to fool someone is what makes it a lie.» What does this tell you about fool? | It means to be silly on the stairs | It means to be very clever | *It means to trick someone with something untrue | It means a kind of pudding
